<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jesuits</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jesuit</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jesuits, or Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, are a Catholic religious order founded in 1540. They were leading missionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Working under Portuguese patronage, they made many converts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> was suppressed there, part of the impact of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ignatius-of-loyola">Ignatius of Loyola</a>, a Basque nobleman of Spanish origin with a military background, discerned his vocation while recovering from a wound received at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>. On 15 August 1534 he and six companions, students at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>, met in a crypt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montmartre">Montmartre</a> to promise poverty, chastity and obedience. They were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-xavier">Francis Xavier</a>, Alfonso Salmerón, Diego Laínez, Nicolás Bobadilla, Peter Faber and Simão Rodrigues. The society was founded in 1540 with the approval of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-iii">Pope Paul III</a>.</p><p>The Jesuits arose just before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a>, but Ignatius and the early Jesuits recognised that the hierarchical church needed reform. They struggled against corruption and spiritual lassitude, and Ignatius insisted on a high level of academic preparation for the clergy. A Jesuit professes perpetual poverty, chastity and obedience and promises special obedience to the pope regarding missions. The order is led for life by a superior general.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Jesuit missionaries established missions around the world from the 16th to the 18th century, with successes and failures. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> the government granted them the fiefdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> in 1580 and removed it in 1587 out of fear of their growing influence. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> their ascendancy grew in the 17th century, with new missions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, Colombia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, and by 1603 there were 345 Jesuit priests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> alone. In some places they protected indigenous peoples from slavers, notably the Guaraní, but elsewhere they enslaved indigenous people after wars in which resistance to European colonisation was defeated.</p><p>The Jesuits were always controversial and often clashed with governments. They were banished from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1594 after Jean Châtel, who had studied at their Collège de Clermont, tried to kill Henri IV, though the ban was soon lifted. The Jesuit scholar Andrew Dial calculated that the order owned more than 20,000 slaves worldwide in 1760, mostly in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, and Jesuits also took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. From 1759 the Church expelled them from most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and its colonies, and on 21 July 1773 Pope Clement XIV suppressed the order. The suppression was lifted in 1814.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Jesuit spirituality draws on the Spiritual Exercises of Ignatius, which aim to find God in all things, and on the Examen, a daily review of the day's events. Formation for the priesthood takes eight to fourteen years. The Decree de genere of 1593 barred anyone of Jewish or Muslim ancestry, contrary to Ignatius's own wishes, and was repealed only in 1946. Jesuit schools, whose studies followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a> under the Ratio Studiorum of 1599, were joined by scientific work in fields from cosmology to seismology, and the order has been described as the most important contributor to experimental physics in the seventeenth century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jesuits” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1549 the king made colonisation a royal enterprise, sending Tomé de Sousa as first governor-general with the first <b>Jesuits</b> under Manuel da Nóbrega.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Society remained strongly Catholic: the dukes rejected the Reformation, brought the <b>Jesuits</b> to Ingolstadt in the 1550s and made Bavaria a centre of the Counter-Reformation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>His ministers, above all du Tillot, pursued an enlightened programme that limited church privileges, and when Pope Clement XIII condemned these measures in the Monitorium of Parma of January 1768, the duchy responded by expelling the <b>Jesuits</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-parma-and-piacenza">Duchy of Parma and Piacenza · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Cultural life drew on several traditions: Francysk Skaryna printed books in Vilnius in the 1520s, the first book in Lithuanian, Martynas Mažvydas's catechism, appeared at Königsberg in 1547, and the <b>Jesuit</b> academy founded in 1579, now Vilnius University, became a leading centre of learning.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Protestantism spread widely in the sixteenth century, but after 1620 the dynasty enforced Catholicism in Bohemia and Austria, confiscated rebel estates and drove tens of thousands of Protestants into exile, while the <b>Jesuits</b> took over schools and universities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the chief power of the Catholic Counter-Reformation: Ignatius of Loyola founded the <b>Society of Jesus</b>, Teresa of Ávila and John of the Cross reformed the Carmelites, and Spanish theologians shaped the Council of Trent.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
